Prototype in JS
A javaScript object is an entity having state and behavior (properties and method).
Js objects have a special property called prototype.
We can set a prototype using __proto__.

Classes in JS
Class is a program-code template for creating objects.
Those objects will have some state(variable) & some behaviour (functions) inside it.

class MyClass{
constructor(){...}
myMethod(){...}
}
Let myObj=new MyClass();

Classes give us a template or blueprint to create an object by which all the objects are the same.

Constructor() method is : 
Automatically invoke by new
We don’t need to call it. It automatically invokes.
Initialize object

Inheritance in JS 
Inheritance is passing down properties & methods from parent class to child class.

Class Parent{
}

Class Child extends parent{
}

If Child & Parents have same method, child’s method will be used.[Method Overriding]super 

super Keyword
The super keyword is used to call the constructor of its parent class to access the parent’s properties and methods.

super(args)

super.parentMethod(args)







🧠 JavaScript Notes

✅ JavaScript Objects & Prototype
A JavaScript object is an entity with:


State → (properties)


Behavior → (methods/functions)


All JavaScript objects have a special internal property called [[Prototype]], which can be accessed using:

 obj.__proto__


This prototype is used for inheritance and method sharing between objects.



🧱 JavaScript Classes
A class in JavaScript is a blueprint/template for creating objects.


Objects created from a class share the same:


State → via variables (properties)


Behavior → via functions (methods)


Syntax:
class MyClass {
  constructor() {
    // Initialization code
  }

  myMethod() {
    // Method logic
  }
}

let myObj = new MyClass(); // Creates an object from the class

constructor() Method:
Special method inside a class.


Automatically invoked when an object is created using new.


Used to initialize object properties.



🔁 Inheritance in JavaScript
Inheritance allows one class (child) to inherit properties and methods from another class (parent).


Syntax:
class Parent {
  parentMethod() {
    console.log("This is the parent method");
  }
}

class Child extends Parent {
  childMethod() {
    console.log("This is the child method");
  }
}

When both parent and child have a method with the same name, the child's method overrides the parent's method. This is known as method overriding.



🗝️ super Keyword
super is used to:


Call the constructor of the parent class.


Access methods and properties from the parent class.


Examples:
class Parent {
  constructor(name) {
    this.name = name;
  }

  greet() {
    console.log(`Hello from ${this.name}`);
  }
}

class Child extends Parent {
  constructor(name, age) {
    super(name); // Calls parent constructor
    this.age = age;
  }

  greet() {
    super.greet(); // Calls parent method
    console.log(`I am ${this.age} years old`);
  }
}


